---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Remorch wallet: a ticket that was three defects"
  description="A swap that took the signature and did nothing turned out to be three separate bugs, one of which hid failures across the whole app."
  path="/work/remorch-app"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">Index</a>

  <h1 class="lede">One ticket, three defects</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    In the wallet PWA, a swap took the user's signature and then did nothing. No message, no
    navigation, no record. The ticket described one bug. The code had three.
  </p>

  <dl class="dossier">
    <div>
      <dt>Surface</dt>
      <dd>Wallet PWA: buy, sell, transfer, swap, credit</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Work</dt>
      <dd>Signing flow rewrite, error categories, login error messages</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Stack</dt>
      <dd>Angular, RxJS, multi-chain signing</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Three bugs stacked up</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The promise was never awaited.</strong> Four order paths started signing inside an
      operator that throws away its return value. The stream finished on the HTTP response, so the
      rejection had nowhere to land.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The global handler then swallowed it.</strong> It unwrapped the error correctly, then
      returned early for anything shaped like an HTTP error. A failed submit has exactly that shape,
      so every failed order in the app produced no message and no telemetry, not just swaps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A swap signs twice, and the second signature was orphaned.</strong> It started fire
      and forget inside the first one's stream, logged errors only to the console, and had no error
      handler. That's the likeliest reason a swap took a signature and did nothing.
    </p>
    <p>Fixing only the path in the ticket would have left the other two live.</p>

    <h2>Rules for the rewrite</h2>
    <p>
      In signing code, normal error habits are dangerous. Attaching the original error, logging it,
      or showing the server's message can all put key material or a signed payload somewhere it
      shouldn't be.
    </p>
    <p>
      So no error object from a signing strategy is ever kept, rethrown, wrapped or shown. Failures
      map to a fixed set of categories, and the category is the whole message. Cancelling is a
      category too: no toast, still navigates. The response cache only stores GET requests, so it
      can't hold a signed payload.
    </p>

    <h2>The review that caught my mistake</h2>
    <p>
      Changes on this path get a dedicated review before merge. Mine came back blocked, with seven
      things to fix and eight to consider. The worst: the new interface still let the caller trigger
      repeated signing without asking the user again. The containment held up. The sequencing was
      wrong, and only a reviewer looking for exactly that would have caught it.
    </p>

    <h2>Login errors, while I was there</h2>
    <p>
      A failed login showed the same two generic toasts for every cause: wrong phrase, wrong
      passcode, rejected account. The phrase step only checked that twelve words were typed. Now
      each cause says what went wrong, once.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p><a class="action" href="https://www.youtube.com/@Remorch">See the app on YouTube</a></p>

  <p><a class="action" href="/work/zhambon">Zhambon, where the deployment is the interesting part</a></p>
</Base>
